TY  - JOUR
ID  - R01362
TI  - Microbial community structure reveals how microaeration improves fermentation during anaerobic co-digestion of brown water and food waste
AU  - Lim, Jun Wei
AU  - Chiam, Jun An
AU  - Wang, Jing-Yuan
PY  - 2014
JO  - Bioresource Technology
VL  - 171
DO  - 10.1016/j.biortech.2014.08.050
AB  - The purpose of this study was to investigate the impact of microaeration on the fermentation process during anaerobic co-digestion of brown water (BW) and food waste (FW). This was achieved by daily monitoring of reactor performance and the determination of its bacterial consortium towards the end of the study. Molecular cloning and sequencing results revealed that bacteria within phyla Firmicutes and Bacteriodetes represented the dominant phylogenetic group. As compared to anaerobic conditions, the fermentation of BW and FW under microaeration conditions gave rise to a significantly more diverse bacterial population and higher proportion of bacterial clones affiliated to the phylum Firmicutes. The acidogenic reactor was therefore able to metabolize a greater variety of substrates leading to higher hydrolysis rates as compared to the anaerobic reactor. Other than enhanced fermentation, microaeration also led to a shift in fermentation production pattern where acetic acid was metabolized for the synthesis of butyric acid.
SP  - 132
EP  - 138
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2014.08.050
KW  - 2 向厌氧水解或产酸阶段供氧
